			<div class="content" id="post-content-3689158">
									

<p>Thanks for the comments.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Click&nbsp;me!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Unclick&nbsp;me!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>how can it be unaffected by the moon's gravity yet itself have a gravitational field</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That's exactly the point. It doesn't make sense, suggesting that the SCP is not a naturally-occurring phenomenon, nor does it abide by natural law. A possible second suggestion is that whatever created it (if it was created) stands outside of natural law as well.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>how can you take a picture of it if it does not interact electromagnetically? how can you take a thermal image of it if it only emits trace amount of particle radiation (and therefore no heat)?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If you can think of a better way to word those parts, I'd be happy to change it. However, the idea was that the object is benign, whereas a typical &quot;real-life&quot; wormhole would be extremely radioactive and dangerous. SCP-3321, however, has minimal to no effect on compasses, and is safe to approach.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>since its entrance is only 3 dimensional, which of the three dimensions does it have? x,y,z, and t are our options; so if it lacks t, then nothing would be able to enter it for starters…</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>how could they possibly know this</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>is this the 2 dimensional part? how can 4 dimensional objects exist and function in a 2 dimensional space? it lacks a direction and time…</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Ultimately, science fiction. However, here are a couple things to consider. First, I specified that the entrance is at least 3-dimensional (can be entered from any direction), so it's basically a spherical hole. The exit, however, is more like a plate, which was described a bit in the exploration logs.</p>
<p>I footnoted the fact some Foundation scientists are exploring hyperconical explanations for this. If you consider that an ordinary cone is a 3-dimensional object with a 2-D base and a 0-D tip, then what if you up the numbers? If SCP-3321 is a 5-dimensional object, then it would have a 4-D entrance and a 2-D exit, and would exist simultaneously in multiple points across space.</p>
<p>In theory.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>that is a pretty damn long tether for that camera</p>
</blockquote>
<p>But not <em>too</em> long. ;)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>clouds are tangible and also very heavy. the average weight of a cumulous cloud is ~1 million pounds.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Technically and technically. The point, however, is that it's thicker than a typical cloud. If you've ever walked outside on a foggy day, even though the air is humid, you don't really feel any resistance as you walk through it. However, the cosmic foam is not like that: it's thick, and you can literally feel it push against you as you navigate through it.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>i find this absolutely ridiculous. there is no way any agency would allow a solo mission into something like a wormhole that is not at all understood. by this time, 5 years have passed but nothing is mentioned about the testing done on the foam samples that have been brought back.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>First off, others have mentioned that they find it unlikely that the Foundation would conduct a solo expedition into an SCP. That's a valid concern, but they've done it before (admittedly more often with D-class). However, in order to change that, I would have to completely redo the third exploration log, which contains the meat of the story. That's not really something I'm comfortable doing at this point.</p>
<p>As for your second point regarding the five years passing, that's a good point as well. I'm not sure if it's too big of a deal, but it's a valid thing to bring up. I could probably add an additional addendum with a timestamp not long after Exploration Log #2. Do you think that's a good idea, or is it not really necessary?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>stahp</p>
</blockquote>
<p>pls</p>
<blockquote>
<p>hmm full transcript, and all the important bits are blacked out. really makes you think.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I wrote &quot;full transcript&quot; because that's the entire transmission, obviously excluding the redaction. In other words, it was short, and that's the whole context. I could probably rewrite &quot;full transcript&quot; into &quot;transcript&quot; or &quot;partial transcript&quot; if that would be clearer. What do you think?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>on what grounds? really gets the brain strained.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I have an answer in my head as to what's behind that redaction, and also why it concerns the Foundation. Some people think it's an annoying omission, but I think unmasking it would hurt the article more than leaving it as is.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<hr />
<p>All that said, again, thanks for the comments, and I'm glad you enjoyed the read!</p>

